1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,759
Argument validation. Problem one often asks you to write a small

2
00:00:03,859 --> 00:00:07,740
function that rejects bad input: the wrong number of arguments, or

3
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:11,540
the wrong types. Here's the toolkit, and a full worked example.

4
00:00:13,633 --> 00:00:17,694
Two different counts. A function's length property is the number of

5
00:00:17,794 --> 00:00:21,792
parameters it declares, but it stops counting at the first default

6
00:00:21,892 --> 00:00:24,525
or rest parameter. So f1's length is just 1.

7
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:28,916
f2 declares three plain parameters, so its length is 3.

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,908
An arrow with only a rest parameter has length zero. Also,

9
00:00:33,008 --> 00:00:36,916
arrows have no arguments object, so they use rest instead.

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,792
arguments dot length is different. It counts how

11
00:00:39,892 --> 00:00:42,564
many values were actually passed in this call.

12
00:00:42,633 --> 00:00:44,405
Let's print them all.

13
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:49,195
1, 3, 0 for the declared lengths. Then 3, 1, 3

14
00:00:49,295 --> 00:00:54,180
for what was passed, and f4's declared length is 2.

15
00:00:57,266 --> 00:01:00,935
Here's the worked example. print_squares takes some text

16
00:01:01,035 --> 00:01:03,358
and a limit, and must reject a wrong

17
00:01:03,458 --> 00:01:06,790
argument count or wrong types, without using isNaN.

18
00:01:06,866 --> 00:01:10,773
Check the count first. If arguments dot length doesn't equal

19
00:01:10,873 --> 00:01:15,046
the function's own length, which is 2, return Invalid Arguments.

20
00:01:15,133 --> 00:01:18,195
Then check each type with typeof. text must be a

21
00:01:18,295 --> 00:01:21,423
string and limit must be a number. Note that it's

22
00:01:21,523 --> 00:01:24,849
OR here: either one being wrong is enough to reject.

23
00:01:24,933 --> 00:01:27,311
Only after both checks pass do we do the

24
00:01:27,411 --> 00:01:30,161
actual work, adding each square to the result.

25
00:01:32,533 --> 00:01:34,569
Let's test it. A valid call.

26
00:01:34,666 --> 00:01:38,382
Too few arguments, wrong types, and too many arguments.

27
00:01:38,466 --> 00:01:40,718
A string 3 instead of a number.

28
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:42,740
And NaN. What happens there?

29
00:01:43,133 --> 00:01:46,302
The good call gives Squares, 1 4 9 16. The next

30
00:01:46,402 --> 00:01:50,823
four are all Invalid Arguments. But NaN slips through and returns

31
00:01:50,923 --> 00:01:54,649
just a, because the loop condition is false right away.

32
00:01:55,733 --> 00:01:59,209
Exam trap. typeof NaN is number, so NaN passes a

33
00:01:59,309 --> 00:02:03,158
typeof check. If you have to reject it without isNaN,

34
00:02:03,258 --> 00:02:07,033
use x not-equal-equal x, which is true only for NaN.

35
00:02:10,133 --> 00:02:13,122
Some questions say to throw instead of returning a

36
00:02:13,222 --> 00:02:16,273
message. Same check, but you throw an error object.

37
00:02:16,366 --> 00:02:18,795
Throwing stops the function immediately, and

38
00:02:18,895 --> 00:02:20,346
the caller has to catch it.

39
00:02:20,733 --> 00:02:26,060
The catch prints TypeError, colon, Invalid Arguments. Read the question carefully

40
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:29,009
to see whether it wants a return or a throw.

41
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:37,085
Default and rest parameters together. pr reports its length, the number

42
00:02:37,185 --> 00:02:41,312
of arguments passed, b, and how many extras landed in rest.

43
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:43,932
We call it with one argument, then with

44
00:02:44,032 --> 00:02:46,700
undefined and two extras, then with null.

45
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:50,900
And rest is a real array, so sum can call reduce on it directly.

46
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:57,447
pr's length is always 1. With one argument, b defaults to 10. Passing undefined

47
00:02:57,547 --> 00:03:02,667
also triggers the default, and rest catches 3 and 4. But null is a

48
00:03:02,767 --> 00:03:06,464
real value, so b is null. And sum gives 0 and 6.

49
00:03:07,533 --> 00:03:10,784
Exam trap. Passing null does not trigger a default

50
00:03:10,884 --> 00:03:14,873
parameter. Only undefined, or leaving the argument out, does.

51
00:03:17,966 --> 00:03:22,182
Practice question 1. Write repeat_word, which takes a word and a

52
00:03:22,282 --> 00:03:27,106
number of times. Return Invalid Arguments unless it's called with exactly

53
00:03:27,206 --> 00:03:31,422
two arguments, a string and a number. Otherwise, return the word

54
00:03:31,522 --> 00:03:35,266
repeated, joined with dashes. Pause the video and try it.

55
00:03:43,566 --> 00:03:47,320
Combine the count check and both typeof checks with OR, and

56
00:03:47,420 --> 00:03:51,369
return Invalid Arguments if any of them fails. Then start with

57
00:03:51,469 --> 00:03:54,374
the word, and add a dash and the word for each

58
00:03:54,474 --> 00:03:57,770
extra repetition. go and 3 gives go dash go dash go.

59
00:04:01,866 --> 00:04:06,086
Practice question 2. What does this print? Pause the video and try it.

60
00:04:14,366 --> 00:04:18,991
1, 1, 4, 0, 2. m's length stops at the first default, y,

61
00:04:19,091 --> 00:04:23,632
so it's 1, even though z comes after. m of 5 passed one

62
00:04:23,732 --> 00:04:29,454
argument, and the next call passed four. n has only a rest parameter,

63
00:04:29,554 --> 00:04:33,082
so its length is 0, and n of 1 2 returns 2.

64
00:04:37,166 --> 00:04:41,463
Recap. A function's length counts declared parameters up to the first

65
00:04:41,563 --> 00:04:45,924
default or rest. arguments dot length counts what was actually passed,

66
00:04:46,024 --> 00:04:49,557
and arrows don't have it. Check the count, then the types

67
00:04:49,657 --> 00:04:53,954
with typeof, and return Invalid Arguments or throw. And remember: NaN

68
00:04:54,054 --> 00:04:57,586
is a number to typeof, and null doesn't trigger defaults.

